WEBVTT

00:00.570 --> 00:03.040
We are faced with a
fairly daunting-looking

00:03.040 --> 00:08.870
indefinite integral of pi
over x natural log of x dx.

00:08.870 --> 00:11.340
Now, what can we
do to address this?

00:11.340 --> 00:14.730
Is u-substitution
a possibility here?

00:14.730 --> 00:16.500
Well for u-substitution,
we want to look

00:16.500 --> 00:19.660
for an expression
and its derivative.

00:19.660 --> 00:25.860
Well, what happens if we set u
equal to the natural log of x?

00:25.860 --> 00:29.280
Now what would du be
equal to in that scenario?

00:29.280 --> 00:32.130
du is going to be the derivative
of the natural log of x

00:32.130 --> 00:37.920
with respect to x,
which is just 1/x dx.

00:37.920 --> 00:40.360
This is an equivalent
statement to saying

00:40.360 --> 00:45.330
that du dx is equal to 1/x.

00:45.330 --> 00:50.020
So do we see a 1/x dx anywhere
in this original expression?

00:50.020 --> 00:51.120
Well, it's kind of hiding.

00:51.120 --> 00:53.520
It's not so obvious, but
this x in the denominator

00:53.520 --> 00:55.250
is essentially a 1/x.

00:55.250 --> 00:57.210
And then that's being
multiplied by a dx.

00:57.210 --> 00:59.010
Let me rewrite this
original expression

00:59.010 --> 01:00.485
to make a little bit more sense.

01:00.485 --> 01:03.110
So the first thing I'm going to
do is I'm going to take the pi.

01:03.110 --> 01:04.651
I should do that in
a different color

01:04.651 --> 01:06.940
since I've already
used-- let me take the pi

01:06.940 --> 01:08.510
and just stick it out front.

01:08.510 --> 01:11.640
So I'm going to stick the pi
out in front of the integral.

01:11.640 --> 01:13.970
And so this becomes
the integral of--

01:13.970 --> 01:17.010
and let me write the 1 over
natural log of x first.

01:17.010 --> 01:22.891
1 over the natural
log of x times 1/x dx.

01:22.891 --> 01:24.390
Now it becomes a
little bit clearer.

01:24.390 --> 01:29.430
These are completely
equivalent statements.

01:29.430 --> 01:31.230
But this makes it
clear that, yes,

01:31.230 --> 01:33.540
u-substitution will
work over here.

01:33.540 --> 01:37.170
If we set our u equal to
natural log of x, then

01:37.170 --> 01:38.980
our du is 1/x dx.

01:44.650 --> 01:46.320
Let's rewrite this integral.

01:46.320 --> 01:52.280
It's going to be equal to pi
times the indefinite integral

01:52.280 --> 01:52.805
of 1/u.

01:55.310 --> 01:59.220
Natural log of x is u-- we
set that equal to natural log

01:59.220 --> 02:01.130
of x-- times du.

02:04.664 --> 02:06.330
Now this becomes
pretty straightforward.

02:06.330 --> 02:08.630
What is the antiderivative
of all of this business?

02:08.630 --> 02:11.210
And we've done very similar
things like this multiple times

02:11.210 --> 02:12.250
already.

02:12.250 --> 02:19.460
This is going to be equal
to pi times the natural log

02:19.460 --> 02:23.580
of the absolute
value of u so that we

02:23.580 --> 02:26.200
can handle even
negative values of u.

02:26.200 --> 02:30.040
The natural log of the
absolute value of u plus c,

02:30.040 --> 02:33.870
just in case we had a
constant factor out here.

02:33.870 --> 02:34.830
And we're almost done.

02:34.830 --> 02:36.860
We just have to
unsubstitute for the u.

02:36.860 --> 02:38.630
u is equal to natural log of x.

02:38.630 --> 02:41.220
So we end up with this kind
of neat-looking expression.

02:41.220 --> 02:42.690
The anti of this
entire indefinite

02:42.690 --> 02:44.050
integral we have simplified.

02:44.050 --> 02:46.110
We have evaluated
it, and it is now

02:46.110 --> 02:53.050
equal to pi times the natural
log of the absolute value of u.

02:53.050 --> 02:55.100
But u is just the
natural log of x.

02:57.860 --> 03:03.220
And then we have this
plus c right over here.

03:03.220 --> 03:05.220
And we could have assumed
that, from the get go,

03:05.220 --> 03:07.680
this original
expression was only

03:07.680 --> 03:10.441
defined for positive
values of x because you had

03:10.441 --> 03:11.940
to take the natural
log here, and it

03:11.940 --> 03:15.070
wasn't an absolute value.

03:15.070 --> 03:17.510
So we can leave this as
just a natural log of x,

03:17.510 --> 03:19.377
but this also works
for the situations

03:19.377 --> 03:21.710
now because we're doing the
absolute value of that where

03:21.710 --> 03:25.220
the natural log of x might
have been a negative number.

03:25.220 --> 03:30.360
For example, if it was a natural
log of 0.5 or, who knows,

03:30.360 --> 03:31.660
whatever it might be.

03:31.660 --> 03:32.980
But then we are all done.

03:32.980 --> 03:36.520
We have simplified
what seemed like a kind

03:36.520 --> 03:38.620
of daunting expression.
